.TH "CLOSEWATCHER.CANCEL_EVENT" "3JS" "March 2, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CloseWatcher.cancel_event \- CloseWatcher: cancel event
.SH SYNOPSIS
A \f[CR]cancel\f[R] event is fired at a \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] object
before the \f[CR]close\f[R] event, so that \f[CR]close\f[R] can be
prevented from firing, if necessary.
It is triggered by all close signals (e.g., the Esc key) as well as
\f[CR]CloseWatcher.requestClose()\f[R].
.SH SYNTAX
Use the event name in methods like \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R], or set
an event handler property.
.IP
.EX
addEventListener(\(dqcancel\(dq, (event) => { })

oncancel = (event) => { }
.EE
.SH EVENT TYPE
An \f[CR]Event\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Using the \f[CR]cancel\f[R] event
In this example, we ask the user to confirm that they really want to
close the component, and if they don\(cqt, we cancel the event using
\f[CR]Event.preventDefault()\f[R], which prevents the \f[CR]close\f[R]
event from being fired.
.IP
.EX
watcher.addEventListener(\(dqcancel\(dq, (e) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]if\f[R] (e.cancelable && hasUnsavedData) {
    \f[B]const\f[R] userReallyWantsToClose = confirm(\(dqAre you sure you want to close?\(dq);
    \f[B]if\f[R] (!userReallyWantsToClose) {
      e.preventDefault();
    }
  }
});

\f[I]// Trigger a close request manually\f[R]
watcher.requestClose();
.EE
